A brivla (a single-word selbri) is identifiable by ending in a vowel other =
than=20
'y', having at least two syllables whose nucleus is a vowel or diphthong no=
t=20
containing 'y', and having two consonants either together or separated by '=
y'.=20
There are corner cases involving cmavo ending in 'y' (e.g. "leky.boi mlatu"=
=20
means "K's cat", "lekyboi mlatu" means "is a cold-ball cat"), but writing=
=20
cmavo ending in 'y' together with both adjacent cmavo (unless they also end=
 in=20
'y' or are "bu"-letterals) is pretty rare.

> By the way as a linguist I find Lojban to be completely opaque in not usi=
ng
> words like verb or noun or adjective.

Brivla are verbs, cmene are nouns, and there are no adjectives or adverbs i=
n=20
Lojban. The equivalent of a noun phrase is an article phrase, as adding an=
=20
article to a verb makes it syntactically like a verb argument (sumti). Comm=
on=20
nouns are expressed by verbs. There is no syntactic difference between "le=
=20
mlatu cu blabi" ("the cat is white") and "le blabi cu mlatu" (*"the white i=
s=20
cat", "the white one is a cat"). There are parts of speech in Lojban that=
=20
don't exist in natlangs that I know of, such as terminators.

